GUIDES = [
dict(slug='guide-event-metrics.html', pillar='analytics',
 title='Virtual Event Metrics That Matter: What to Measure and How to Read It | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='The metrics that tell you whether a virtual event worked (peak and average concurrency, watch time, drop-off curve, interaction rates, on-demand views), how platforms define them, and the vanity numbers to ignore.',
 h1='Metrics that matter: <span class="em">reading the numbers honestly.</span>', h1_plain='Metrics that matter: reading the numbers honestly',
 lede='Every platform gives you a dashboard. Most of it is noise. The handful of numbers that describe whether your event worked, how they\'re defined, and how to read them.',
 related=['guide-measuring-roi.html','guide-post-event-report.html','guide-engagement-features.html'],
 body='''
<p>The morning after an event, someone asks "how did it go?" and the answer is usually a number chosen because it's the biggest. Registrations. "Views". Total minutes. None of those tells you whether the event did its job. These are the metrics we report, what they mean, and the traps in each.</p>
<h2>Registered, attended, no-show rate</h2>
<p>Attended divided by registered. Free virtual events typically see 35–55% attendance; paid and internal mandatory events much higher. A low rate points at reminders and timing, not content. Report the rate, not just the registrations.</p>
<h2>Peak concurrent viewers</h2>
<p>The largest number watching at one moment. It's the honest measure of live reach and the number to size infrastructure on next time. Different platforms and simulcast destinations count it differently. Agree a definition before the show and note it in the report.</p>
<h2>Average concurrent viewers and the attendance curve</h2>
<p>More useful than the peak: the shape of the audience over time. Plot concurrent viewers against the run order. The curve shows which sessions held people and which lost them, and where the break went on too long. This graph is the most valuable thing in a post-event report and the one most platforms hide.</p>
<h2>Average watch time and completion</h2>
<p>How long the typical attendee stayed, and what share saw the end. For a 90-minute town hall, 60+ minutes average is strong. Watch time is the metric most sensitive to production quality. Bad audio shows up here first.</p>
<h2>Drop-off points</h2>
<p>Where people left. Cross-reference with the run order: a cliff after a 25-minute unscripted speech is a content note; a cliff at the same moment on every device is a technical fault. Both are worth knowing.</p>
<h2>Interaction rates</h2>
<p>Questions per 100 attendees, poll response rate, reactions per minute, chat participants. Reported as rates, not totals, so events of different sizes can be compared.</p>
<h2>On-demand views in the following 30 days</h2>
<p>For many events the on-demand audience exceeds the live one. Report it, and report watch time on it too. An on-demand "view" is often 20 seconds.</p>
<h2>The vanity numbers</h2>
<p><strong>"Views"</strong> on social platforms (counted after 3 seconds). <strong>Total minutes watched</strong> without the number of people (a huge number that means nothing). <strong>Impressions</strong>. <strong>Registrations</strong> without attendance. Use them for reach context, never as headline outcomes.</p>
<h2>Setting the baseline</h2>
<p>Numbers mean more against a comparison: last year's event, the industry norm, or the target you set in planning. Agree the targets before the show so the post-event conversation is about results, not definitions. The <a href="guide-post-event-report.html">report template</a> has a place for them.</p>
<table><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Good sign</th><th>Warning sign</th></tr>
<tr><td>Attendance rate</td><td>&gt;45% (free)</td><td>&lt;30%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Avg watch time (90-min event)</td><td>&gt;55 min</td><td>&lt;30 min</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completion</td><td>&gt;40%</td><td>&lt;20%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Questions per 100 attendees</td><td>&gt;5</td><td>&lt;1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poll response rate</td><td>&gt;40%</td><td>&lt;15%</td></tr>
<tr><td>On-demand views / live attendees</td><td>&gt;0.5</td><td>&lt;0.1</td></tr></table>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-measuring-roi.html', pillar='analytics',
 title='How to Measure Virtual Event ROI: A Model That Finance Will Accept | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='A practical ROI model for virtual and hybrid events: full cost including internal time, outcome value by event type (leads, pipeline, retention, cost avoided), attribution windows and the comparison to the in-person alternative.',
 h1='Measuring ROI: <span class="em">a model finance will accept.</span>', h1_plain='Measuring ROI: a model finance will accept',
 lede='Virtual events are cheaper than in-person and easier to measure, yet most organisers can\'t say what theirs returned. A model that works for marketing, internal comms and training events.',
 related=['guide-event-metrics.html','guide-post-event-report.html','guide-live-streaming-cost.html'],
 body='''
<p>"ROI" gets thrown around events as a synonym for "was it good". Finance means something narrower: what did it cost, what did it return, and how do you know. Virtual events can answer all three better than almost any other marketing or communications activity, if the model is set up before the show.</p>
<h2>1. Count the full cost</h2>
<p>Production (crew, kit, studio, platform), content (design, video, speakers), promotion, and, the one everyone omits, internal time: the hours your team spent planning, rehearsing and following up, at a loaded day rate. A "£6,000 event" is often a £15,000 event once internal time is in. Use the honest number; it makes the return more credible, not less.</p>
<h2>2. Define the outcome by event type</h2>
<p><strong>Lead generation / marketing:</strong> qualified leads generated, opportunities created within an attribution window (typically 90 days), pipeline value, and (where the sales cycle allows) closed revenue. Value = pipeline × historical win rate, or closed revenue where available.</p>
<p><strong>Customer events:</strong> retention or expansion among attendees vs a matched group of non-attendees; product adoption of features demonstrated; support tickets avoided.</p>
<p><strong>Internal communications:</strong> reach (share of workforce who watched live or on demand), comprehension (a three-question pulse survey afterwards), and the cost avoided versus the in-person alternative. Travel, venues, lost working days.</p>
<p><strong>Training:</strong> completion, assessment scores, and the cost per trained person versus classroom delivery.</p>
<h2>3. Attribute honestly</h2>
<p>Events rarely cause a sale on their own. Use the same attribution approach the rest of marketing uses (first touch, multi-touch, or influenced pipeline) and label it. "Influenced £400k of pipeline" with the method stated beats "generated £400k" with a raised eyebrow from finance.</p>
<h2>4. Compare with the alternative</h2>
<p>The most persuasive ROI statement is comparative: the virtual conference reached 2,400 people for £18,000; the in-person version reached 400 for £95,000. Cost per attendee, cost per lead, cost per trained employee. These are the numbers that get next year's budget approved. Our <a href="guide-live-streaming-cost.html">cost guide</a> helps build the virtual side.</p>
<h2>5. Put a value on the content</h2>
<p>A well-produced event generates a keynote video, session recordings, highlight clips and social cuts that would cost real money to make separately. Value them at replacement cost and include the on-demand audience in reach. The <a href="guide-on-demand-repurposing.html">repurposing guide</a> shows how to maximise it.</p>
<h2>A worked example</h2>
<table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Production, platform, promotion</td><td>£12,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Internal time (30 days × £450)</td><td>£13,500</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Full cost</strong></td><td><strong>£25,500</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Attendees live / on demand (30 days)</td><td>1,100 / 900</td></tr>
<tr><td>Qualified leads (90 days)</td><td>85</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opportunities × avg value × win rate</td><td>22 × £30,000 × 25% = £165,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Content replacement value</td><td>£9,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Return / cost</strong></td><td><strong>6.8×</strong> (influenced-pipeline basis)</td></tr></table>
<div class="callout"><p>Decide the model, the attribution window and the comparison event <em>before</em> the show. Retrofitting an ROI story afterwards is exactly what finance is trained to distrust.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-on-demand-repurposing.html', pillar='analytics',
 title='After the Live Event: On-Demand, Highlights & Repurposing Content | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to turn a live event into months of content: the on-demand edit, chaptering, highlight reels and social cuts, captions and transcripts, gated vs open access, and the 30-day distribution plan.',
 h1='After the live: <span class="em">the event is the raw material.</span>', h1_plain='After the live: the event is the raw material',
 lede='The live audience is often the smaller one. How to edit, package and distribute the event so it keeps working for weeks, and what to ask the production team to record.',
 related=['guide-event-metrics.html','guide-measuring-roi.html','guide-encoders-bitrates.html'],
 body='''
<p>An event that ends when the stream stops has wasted most of what it made. The recordings, the questions, the moments (packaged well and released on a schedule) reach more people than the live show and keep delivering for the quarter. This is the process, starting with what to record.</p>
<h2>Record for the edit, not just the archive</h2>
<p>Ask for: a clean <strong>programme recording</strong> at high bitrate (not the compressed stream); <strong>ISO recordings</strong> of each camera and each remote guest, so the editor can re-cut; the <strong>graphics-free version</strong> (or a separate graphics track) so lower thirds can be corrected; and the <strong>audio stems</strong>. Tell the production team before the show. It changes how they set up the recorders. The <a href="guide-encoders-bitrates.html">encoder guide</a> explains the quality difference.</p>
<h2>The on-demand edit: same day if possible</h2>
<p>Trim the pre-show and breaks, fix any live wobbles, correct captions, and publish within 24 hours while the event is still being talked about. Chapter it by session or topic so people can jump to what they need; an unchaptered three-hour recording is barely watched.</p>
<h2>Highlights and social cuts</h2>
<p>A 90-second highlight reel for the website and LinkedIn; four to eight 30–60-second vertical clips of the best moments (a strong quote, a demo, a laugh) for social; and a 10–15-minute "if you missed it" version of the keynote. Prioritise moments over messages: the clip that travels is the human one. Deliver them within 48 hours; a week later they're history.</p>
<h2>Captions, transcripts and the written layer</h2>
<p>Corrected captions on every video (accessibility and search). A full transcript on the event page. A written summary or blog post per session, which is what search engines actually index. Pull quotes for social. This layer costs little and is where most of the long-tail audience arrives.</p>
<h2>Gated or open?</h2>
<p>Gating the on-demand version behind registration captures leads but cuts viewing by a large factor. A common compromise: keynote and highlights open (reach), full sessions gated (leads). For internal events, open to staff via SSO, with a reminder campaign.</p>
<h2>The 30-day distribution plan</h2>
<table><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr>
<tr><td>Day 0–1</td><td>Thank-you email with on-demand link; highlight reel on social</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day 2–5</td><td>Social clips daily; written session summaries published</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 2</td><td>Best session promoted to non-attendees; speaker shares their clip</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 3</td><td>Q&amp;A follow-up: unanswered questions answered in a post or short video</td></tr>
<tr><td>Week 4</td><td>Report on-demand numbers; decide what becomes evergreen content</td></tr></table>
<h2>Measure it</h2>
<p>On-demand views and watch time, clip reach, transcript page visits, leads from gated content. Report them alongside the live numbers (see <a href="guide-event-metrics.html">metrics</a>) and the event's ROI story roughly doubles.</p>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-post-event-report.html', pillar='analytics',
 title='The Post-Event Report: A Template Leadership Will Actually Read | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to write a virtual event post-event report: the one-page summary, results against targets, the attendance curve, what worked and what didn\'t, technical incidents, costs, and recommendations for next time.',
 h1='The post-event report: <span class="em">one page, then the evidence.</span>', h1_plain='The post-event report: one page, then the evidence',
 lede='The report that gets next year\'s budget approved is short, honest and structured. The template we use, and a download.',
 related=['guide-event-metrics.html','guide-measuring-roi.html','guide-when-it-goes-wrong.html'],
 body='''
<p>Most post-event reports are a platform dashboard export stapled to a list of thanks. Nobody reads them, so nothing changes, so next year's event has the same problems. This structure takes an afternoon to write and gets read because page one answers the only question leadership has: did it work, and what do we do differently?</p>
<h2>Page one: the summary</h2>
<p>Five short sections. <strong>Objective</strong>: one sentence, as agreed before the event. <strong>Result</strong>: the two or three headline metrics against their targets, with a plain verdict. <strong>What worked</strong>: three bullets. <strong>What didn't</strong>: three bullets, including anything the audience would have noticed. <strong>Recommendation</strong>: what to repeat, what to change, and the decision you need. If they read nothing else, this page is enough.</p>
<h2>Results against targets</h2>
<p>A table: each agreed metric, target, actual, last event's number. Attendance rate, peak and average concurrency, watch time, completion, interaction rates, on-demand views, and the outcome metric (leads, pulse-survey score, completions). Definitions in a footnote. The <a href="guide-event-metrics.html">metrics guide</a> explains each.</p>
<h2>The attendance curve</h2>
<p>One chart: concurrent viewers over time, with the run order marked along the bottom. Annotate the moments. The keynote, the break, the drop-off. It's the single most persuasive image in the document and the one most likely to change next year's agenda.</p>
<h2>Audience feedback</h2>
<p>Survey results (keep the survey to five questions), representative comments, and the questions that couldn't be answered live. Quote the critical ones; a report that only quotes praise isn't believed.</p>
<h2>Technical and production notes</h2>
<p>Any incidents, in the honest one-line format from the <a href="guide-when-it-goes-wrong.html">recovery playbook</a>: what happened, audience impact, what's been changed. What the production team recommends for next time. Connectivity, rehearsal time, crew. This section is where the case for a proper budget is made without arguing.</p>
<h2>Costs and ROI</h2>
<p>Full cost including internal time, cost per attendee, and the outcome value on the basis agreed. See <a href="guide-measuring-roi.html">measuring ROI</a>. Compared with the in-person equivalent where relevant.</p>
<h2>Content and follow-up</h2>
<p>What's been published, where, and how it's performing. The on-demand numbers to date.</p>
<h2>Recommendations and decisions needed</h2>
<p>Specific, dated, owned. "Book a rehearsal day (production, by March)", "Move the break to 45 minutes (agenda owner)", "Approve human captioning (£1,800, finance)". Recommendations without owners are wishes.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>The <a href="templates.html">post-event report template</a> is on the templates page, with the metrics table pre-built.</p></div>
'''),
]
